===Etymology 2===
* {{sense|mathematics, imaginary number}} abbreviation of {{m|en|imaginary}}
* {{sense|engineering, electric current}} abbreviation of French {{m|fr|intensité du courant}} first used by M. André-Marie Ampère
* {{sense|computer programming, generic index}} abbreviation of {{m|en|index}}

====Pronunciation====
{{wikipedia|Close front unrounded vowel}}
* {{audio|mul|Close front unrounded vowel.ogg|IPA}}

====Symbol====
{{head|mul|symbols}}

# {{lb|mul|mathematics|often in italics or bold}} The [[imaginary unit]]; a fixed [[square root]] of -1. Graphically, <math>i</math> is shown on the vertical (y-axis) plane.
#: {{syn|mul|j}}
#: {{ux|en|a+bi with a is real part and b is imaginary part}}
# {{lb|mul|engineering|often in bold}} The [[current]] flow in an electric [[circuit]], frequently measured in [[ampere]]s.
#: {{ux|en|2=v=ir (Ohm's Law)}}
# {{lb|mul|mathematics|programming}} A common variable name representing a generic index, especially in loops.
#: {{syn|mul|j}}
# {{lb|mul|IPA|romanization}} a {{w|close front unrounded vowel}}.
# {{lb|mul|superscript {{angbr IPA|ⁱ}}|IPA}} {{IPAfont|[i]}}-coloring, an {{IPAfont|[i]}} on-glide or off-glide (a diphthong), or a weak, fleeting, epenthetic or echo {{IPAfont|[i]}}.
# {{lb|mul|ISO}} transliterates Indic [[इ]] (or equivalent).
# {{lb|mul|financial mathematics}} [[annual]] [[effective]] [[interest rate]]

===Etymology 3===
Lower case form of upper case Roman numeral [[I]], apparently derived from the shape of a notch scored across a tally stick.

