{{head|cy|pronouns|cat2=personal pronouns}}

# [[I]], [[me]]

=====See also=====
* {{l|cy|fi||I, me}}
* {{l|cy|mi||I, me}}

===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|cy|wlm|y}}, from {{inh|cy|owl|di}} (pronounced {{IPAchar|/ði/}}), from {{inh|cy|cel-pro|*dū}}, related to {{cog|br|da||to, for}}, {{cog|kw|dhe||to, for}}, {{cog|ga|do||to, for}}.

====Preposition====
{{head|cy|prepositions|triggers soft mutation}}

# [[to]], [[into]] (a place)
#: {{ux|cy|Aethon nhw '''i'''ʼr ysbyty.|They went '''to''' the hospital.}}
# [[for]] (a recipient)
#: {{ux|cy|Mae’r jem '''i''' Siân.|The jewel is '''for''' Siân.}}
#: {{ux|cy|Dw i’n prynu teiar newydd '''i'''’n car.|I'm buying a new tyre '''for''' our car.}}
# [[that]]
#: {{ux|cy|Maen nhw’n dweud '''iddi''' hi yfed gormod o gwrw.|They say '''that''' she drank too much beer.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* {{m|cy||I}} is often used to indicate direction "to" a place or "(in order) to" do an action in contrast to {{m|cy|at}}, which indicates direction "to" a person.
** {{uxi|cy|Rwy'n mynd '''i''''r feddygfa.|I'm going '''to''' the surgery.}}
** {{uxi|cy|Rwy'n mynd '''i''' weld y meddyg.|I'm going '''to''' see the surgery.}}
** {{uxi|cy|Rwy'n mynd '''at''' y meddyg.|I'm going '''to''' the doctor.}}
: See {{m|cy|o}} for a similar distinction for "from".
* The literary language distinguishes between unemphatic personal forms and personal forms with emphasis on the pronoun.
** {{uxi|cy|Rhaid '''inni''' fynd.|'''We''' must go. (no particular emphasis)}}
** {{uxi|cy|Rhaid '''i ni''' fynd.|'''We''' must go. (emphasis on ''we'')}}
: In less formal language, this distinction is not made in writing.
:* {{uxi|cy|Rhaid '''i ni''' fynd.|'''We''' must go. (no particular emphasis)}}
* Used as a [[preterite|preterite tense]] form of ‘that’. The subject moves to the front of the subordinate clause, directly following ''i'', and the verb changes back to its verbal noun form.

